# L0 flash_area timing benchmark — Kconfig entry point.

menu "Zephyr"
source "Kconfig.zephyr"
endmenu

module = APP_PERF_L0
module-str = APP_PERF_L0
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_config"

config APP_PERF_L0_MAX_XFER
	int "Largest transfer size swept, in bytes"
	default 65536
	range 16 262144
	help
	  Upper end of the read/write size sweep, clamped at runtime to one
	  erase block. Sets the size of the app's single static buffer, so it
	  is also the app's RAM cost — lower it on a small-RAM board.

	  The layers above read and write whole sectors during compaction, so
	  a model fitted from a sweep that stops short of the erase block has
	  to extrapolate for exactly those operations.

config APP_PERF_L0_REGION_BLOCKS
	int "Erase blocks the benchmark may destroy"
	default 32
	range 2 1024
	help
	  The benchmark works in the first N erase blocks of
	  storage_partition and erases them repeatedly. Everything in them is
	  lost. Clamped at runtime to the partition's block count.

	  This also bounds the erase-size sweep, which doubles its span up to
	  half this number, and the write sweep, which may not write past it.

config APP_PERF_L0_TARGET_MS
	int "Target duration of one sweep point, in milliseconds"
	default 200
	range 1 10000
	help
	  Each read/write sweep point repeats its operation until roughly this
	  much time has passed, so that the cycle counter's resolution and any
	  one-off jitter are negligible against the batch. Raising it buys
	  precision at a linear cost in run time.

config APP_PERF_L0_MAX_REPS
	int "Repetition ceiling for one sweep point"
	default 4096
	range 1 1000000
	help
	  Bounds the batch for operations so cheap that TARGET_MS would ask
	  for millions of them — on the flash simulator a small read is
	  nanoseconds, and without this the sweep would never finish.

config APP_PERF_L0_ERASE_REPS
	int "How many times each erase span is repeated"
	default 5
	range 1 100
	help
	  Erase time on NOR varies with wear and with the block's contents, so
	  a span is erased several times and the spread is reported alongside
	  the mean. Each repetition costs a full erase of the span — on a part
	  with 1 s block erases and a 16-block span, that is 16 s per
	  repetition.

config APP_PERF_L0_PROGRAM_PAGE
	int "Program-page size of the part, in bytes"
	default 256
	range 0 65536
	help
	  The unit a NOR part programs in — 256 B on the MX25R64 and on most
	  SPI NOR. It is not discoverable through the flash_area API, which
	  reports the erase block and the write alignment but not this, so it
	  has to be told.

	  It is used only to choose sizes for the page-program sweep, which
	  pins each transfer to a page boundary so the staircase in write cost
	  is visible directly rather than averaged away. Set it to 0 to skip
	  that phase; setting it wrong costs nothing but a badly chosen set of
	  sample points.

config APP_PERF_L0_STEPS_PER_OCTAVE
	int "Size samples per doubling"
	default 8
	range 1 8
	help
	  How finely the read/write size sweep subdivides each octave: 1 gives
	  bare powers of two, 2 adds the 1.5x midpoints, 4 gives 1x 1.25x 1.5x
	  1.75x, 8 halves the gaps again.

	  This is the resolution knob, and resolution is the point. A cost step
	  between two samples is only known to lie somewhere in that interval,
	  so bare powers of two place a feature within a factor of two — enough
	  to know something happened, never enough to say where. Octaves below
	  `write_align * this` carry fewer points, since the subdivision would
	  otherwise ask for a sub-alignment stride.

	  Cost is roughly linear in this for reads (milliseconds) and steeply
	  so for writes, which erase the space each point consumes.

config APP_PERF_L0_PASSES
	int "Timed passes per sweep point"
	default 3
	range 1 10
	help
	  How many times each read/write sweep point repeats its whole timed
	  batch. The reported cost is the mean over all passes; the extremes
	  are reported beside it as a spread, and emitted as min_ns/max_ns on
	  the machine-readable row.

	  One pass gives a number with nothing to say about its own
	  reliability. Three make a point whose passes disagree visible as
	  such, which matters because the fit weights every point equally and
	  a single disturbed batch would otherwise tilt the model silently.

	  Reads cost TARGET_MS per extra pass. Writes also consume erased
	  space, which the region cursor pays for in whole-region erases.
